{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A psalm of David. The utterance of YHWH to my master.</b> It is fitting to interpret this psalm about Abraham, on account of the episode of the king of Salem [Melchizedek]; only the difficulty for us is Zion, for it was David's city, and we can fix it by a somewhat forced route. And what seems correct to me: this psalm was composed by one of the poets about David, like the psalm 'May YHWH answer you on the day of trouble,' 'YHWH, in your strength the king rejoices,' and also for Solomon, 'Elohim, give your judgments to the king.' And this psalm was composed when the men of David swore, saying: 'You shall not go out with us to battle.' And this is the beginning of the poet's words: 'The utterance of YHWH to my master' — he being David." "<b>Sit at my right hand.</b> And the sense of 'sit' — in my house, and serve me." "And the sense of 'at my right hand' — for the right hand performs valor. And behold, the sense is: you shall not go out with us to battle, for the right hand of HaShem will fight on your behalf, and He will make your enemies a footstool for your feet — they shall be brought before you for you to trample them."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 110, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:39:22.210556010Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}